      "side": 1,
      "message": "Glob only adds dependencies for directory changes that could cause the result to change. But, it doesn\u0027t add a dependency in this case, since it\u0027s expected that you\u0027ll be adding a dependency of your own for the processing that you\u0027re doing. Since this is doing the opposite -- not doing anything if we find it, then this change is needed. At least that\u0027s the rough explanation I got from Colin.\n\nBut some of the glob test cases don\u0027t fully agree with that -- I\u0027d expect \u0027a/a/a\u0027 and \u0027[a]/a/a\u0027 to have similar dependencies (even if we don\u0027t optimize the single-option case).\n\nWhen that file exists, the non-wild version doesn\u0027t contain any dependencies, where the wild one will return \u0027.\u0027, \u0027a\u0027, \u0027a/a\u0027. The necessary dependency in either case would be \u0027a/a/a\u0027, though \u0027a/a\u0027 would work. With low optimizations, then \u0027.\u0027 and \u0027a\u0027 would be understandable.\n\nHmm, and this change would force a ninja file rebuild on any modification of the Android.mk too, where it really only needs to rebuild if the file appears or disappears.",
